   116	| `git rebase -i` on pushed history | Rewrites pushed history | New commits |
   117	| `git branch -D` on unmerged branches | Discards branch tip irreversibly | `git branch -d` (only succeeds if merged) |
   118	| `git stash` inside a worktree | Stash is per-worktree but easy to forget / lose if worktree is force-removed — never use stash to defer work across delegation boundaries | Create a temporary linked worktree (`git worktree add -b emergency-fix <path> <base>`), do the work, commit, then remove the temp worktree. Per `git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree`. |
   119	| `gh pr close` without merge | Discards reviewed work | Either merge or convert to draft |
   120	| `gh issue delete` | GitHub does not support undelete | `gh issue close` + comment explaining |
   121	| `git worktree remove --force <path>` / `git worktree remove -f <path>` | Discards any uncommitted changes inside the worktree without review; work staged in the worktree (notes, edits, partial commits) is permanently lost | Run `git status` inside the worktree first to confirm a clean tree AND that the branch is merged into base; then use standard `git worktree remove <path>` (no `--force`). If unclean: commit, discard, or escalate to user via AskUserQuestion before removal. |
   122	| Subagent: `git push` / `gh pr *` / `gh issue *` | Bypasses manager's integration authority | Subagent reports `DONE`; manager handles |
   123	
   124	**Cross-layer drift is not yet detected automatically.** Until issue #258 lands, every PR that touches multiple layers (e.g., agent docs + runtime specs + plugin agents) must be hand-reviewed for drift via adversarial review per `evaluation/SKILL.md`. See issue #258 for the planned validator.
   125	
   126	**Safe-list exceptions** — these patterns may LOOK destructive but are routine and require no confirmation:
   127	
   128	| Pattern | Why safe |
   129	|---|---|
   130	| `rm -rf node_modules/` | Routine clean-and-reinstall |
   131	| `rm -rf dist/` / `build/` / `.next/` / `__pycache__/` / `.cache/` / `.turbo/` / `coverage/` | Routine build artifact cleanup |
   132	| `git worktree remove <path>` after merge | Lifecycle cleanup per Procedure P5 |
   133	| `git branch -d <merged-branch>` | Safe-delete (`-d` lowercase) only succeeds on merged branches |
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	## Procedures
   138	
   139	Numbered procedures the manager runs during a git-active session. Subagents execute Procedure P3 (commit) inside their delegated worktree; everything else is manager-only.
   140	
   141	### P1 — Verify prerequisites
   142	
   143	At session start when the user selects "Git workflow (worktree + PR)":
   144	
   145	1. Run `gh --version` to confirm CLI availability.
   146	2. Run `gh auth status` to confirm authentication.
   147	3. Run `git remote get-url origin` to confirm the remote is configured.
   148	4. Run `git ls-remote --heads origin <base-branch>` to confirm the base branch exists on the remote.
   149	5. Run `git check-ignore -q .gobbi/projects/<name>/worktrees/` to confirm the worktree directory is gitignored.
   150	
   151	If any **Critical** prerequisite fails, fall back to Direct commit mode. If any **Warning** prerequisite fails, inform the user and continue (or remediate per their choice).
   152	
   153	### P2 — Create worktree
   154	
   155	For each task entering Execution:
   156	
   157	1. **Sync the base branch** — `git checkout <base-branch> && git pull --ff-only` to ensure the worktree branches from the up-to-date base.
   158	2. **Re-verify base branch on remote** — `git ls-remote --heads origin <base-branch>` (the base may have been deleted between session start and now).
   159	3. **Create the worktree** — `git worktree add -b <branch-name> .gobbi/projects/<name>/worktrees/<branch-name> <base-branch>`. Branch name follows the regex in [`conventions.md` § Branch Naming](conventions.md#branch-naming).
   160	4. **Install dependencies in the worktree** — each worktree has its own working directory; package managers, virtual environments, and build caches are not shared. Run the project's install command (e.g., `bun install`, `npm ci`) before delegating.
   161	5. **Pass the absolute worktree path** to every delegation prompt that operates on this task.
   162	
   163	### P3 — Delegate within worktree (subagent procedure)
   164	
   165	The subagent's first action is to `cd` to the worktree's absolute path. From there, the subagent follows the standard Study → Plan → Execute → Verify → Commit lifecycle (per [`execution/SKILL.md`](../execution/SKILL.md)). Per-task commit rules:
   166	
   167	- **Commit only after Verify passes** — never commit unverified work.
   168	- **One focused commit per subtask** — commit grammar per [`conventions.md` § Commit Messages](conventions.md#commit-messages).
   169	- **Commit message footer carries `AI-Provenance-Record:`** per [`conventions.md` § Commit Trailers](conventions.md#commit-trailers).
   170	- **Never push** — the manager owns push.
   171	
   172	### P4 — Push and open PR
   173	
   174	After all subtasks for the issue are complete and verified:
   175	
   176	1. `cd` to the worktree path.
   177	2. **Push the branch** — `git push -u origin <branch-name>`.
   178	3. **Open the PR** with `gh pr create` — title and body follow [`conventions.md` § Pull Request Format](conventions.md#pull-request-format).
   179	4. **Apply type label** — `gh pr edit <num> --add-label <type>` per [`conventions.md` § Label Registry](conventions.md#label-registry).
   180	5. **Monitor CI** — `gh pr checks <num> --watch` (or equivalent for external CI).
   181	
   182	### P5 — Land PR
   183	
   184	**Pre-merge gate** — all preconditions must hold before merging:
   185	
   186	- [ ] All CI checks green on the PR branch HEAD
   187	- [ ] All subtasks marked done in the plan
   188	- [ ] No subagent has uncommitted changes in the worktree
   189	- [ ] PR body has all required sections per [`conventions.md` § Pull Request Format](conventions.md#pull-request-format)
   190	- [ ] If targeting a non-default branch: note linked issues that the manager must close manually post-merge (closing keywords only trigger on default-branch merges)
   191	
   192	If any precondition fails, do not merge — surface the failing item to the user.
   193	
   194	**Merge sequence** (all preconditions pass):
   195	
   196	1. `gh pr merge <num> --squash --delete-branch` — atomic squash merge + remote branch deletion.
   197	2. `git checkout <base-branch> && git pull --ff-only` — sync the local base branch with the merge.
   198	3. Before removing the worktree: run `git status` inside it to confirm a clean working tree AND that the branch is merged into base. Then `git worktree remove .gobbi/projects/<name>/worktrees/<branch-name>`. **Never use `--force` / `-f` without explicit user approval via AskUserQuestion** — force-remove silently discards any uncommitted work inside the worktree (Forbidden Operations). If the status is unclean, commit or discard explicitly before removal.
   199	4. `git worktree prune` — clean stale references.
   200	5. **Clean up empty parent directories** — nested branch names like `feat/42-x` create `worktrees/feat/` parent dirs; `git worktree remove` only removes the leaf. Run `find .gobbi/projects/<name>/worktrees/ -type d -empty -delete`.
   201	6. **If non-default-branch PR**: close linked issues — `gh issue close <num> -c "Closed by PR #<pr-num>"` for each.
   202	
   203	### P6 — Recover orphaned worktree
   204	
   205	When `gh pr list` or `git worktree list` surfaces an orphaned worktree (left from a crashed or abandoned session):
   206	
   207	1. **Inspect** — `cd <orphaned-worktree> && git log -3` to see what's there.
   208	2. **Surface to manager** via `NEEDS_CONTEXT` with a `user-question:` block: "Found orphaned worktree at `<path>` with N commits — recover the work, resume from here, or clean up?" — the manager presents this to the user via AskUserQuestion.
   209	3. **If recover** — continue from the existing commits (skip P2; jump to P3 if work needs to continue, or P4 if work is complete).
   210	4. **If clean up** — `git worktree remove <path>` + `git worktree prune` + cleanup empty parent dirs.
   211	
   212	### P7 — Handle CI failure
   213	
   214	When a PR's CI fails:
   215	
   216	1. **Identify the failed run** — `gh run list --branch <branch-name> --limit 5`.
   217	2. **View logs** — `gh run view <run-id> --log-failed`.
   218	   - For external CI (CircleCI, Jenkins, etc.), `gh` returns nothing useful — surface the status check URL from the PR for the user to inspect.
   219	3. **Diagnose root cause** from the logs.
   220	4. **Fix in the worktree** — delegate to executor with the diagnosis as scope context; executor commits the fix.

